analysisWe need to produce an assert statement reflecting the comment.

Comment: 
```
// @@@ If fromIndex is non-negative and toIndex is greater than or equal to fromIndex, then for every index i from fromIndex (inclusive) to toIndex (exclusive), the value of the bit at index i is equal to the specified value.
```

Method signature: public void set(int fromIndex, int toIndex, boolean value)

Implementation: if (value) set(fromIndex, toIndex); else clear(fromIndex, toIndex);

So spec: If fromIndex >=0 && toIndex >= fromIndex, then for all i, fromIndex <= i < toIndex, this.get(i) == value.

We need to use \forall int i; condition; spec. Use this.get(i). Should we reference old? Not needed because after operation we assert bits are set accordingly, no need old values.

Thus assert: assert (fromIndex >= 0 && toIndex >= fromIndex) => \forall int i; i >= fromIndex && i < toIndex; this.get(i) == value;

But need parentheses to ensure grouping: (fromIndex >= 0 && toIndex >= fromIndex) => (\forall int i; i >= fromIndex && i < toIndex; this.get(i) == value);

Check syntax: The spec uses => binary with lower priority than && and ||. So we need parentheses around the forall expression? The => expects two operands: left premise, right consequence. Right consequence is the forall expression. So:

assert (fromIndex >= 0 && toIndex >= fromIndex) => \forall int i; i >= fromIndex && i < toIndex; this.get(i) == value;

Make sure semicolons: \forall int i; condition; spec

Thus final code:

```java
assert (fromIndex >= 0 && toIndex >= fromIndex) => \forall int i; i >= fromIndex && i < toIndex; this.get(i) == value;
```

Check formatting: need <code> block.

assistantfinal<code>assert (fromIndex >= 0 && toIndex >= fromIndex) => \forall int i; i >= fromIndex && i < toIndex; this.get(i) == value;</code>
